The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
	usb_fill_bulk_urb(urb, dev->udev, usb_rcvbulkpipe(dev->udev, 2),
			  urb->transfer_buffer, RX_BUFFER_SIZE,
			  ems_usb_read_bulk_callback, dev);

	retval = usb_submit_urb(urb, GFP_ATOMIC);
```
Reason: The URB was not re-anchored to `dev->rx_submitted` before resubmission. The USB core automatically unanchors the URB before invoking the completion callback. Without re-anchoring, the URB is no longer part of the anchor list after resubmission. When `ems_usb_close()` calls `usb_kill_anchored_urbs()`, it only frees anchored URBs, so the completed and resubmitted URBs are never freed, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
	usb_anchor_urb(urb, &dev->rx_submitted);

	retval = usb_submit_urb(urb, GFP_ATOMIC);
	if (!retval)
		return;

	usb_unanchor_urb(urb);
```
Reason: `usb_anchor_urb` adds the URB back to the `dev->rx_submitted` anchor list before submission, ensuring it remains tracked. If submission succeeds (`retval == 0`), the URB stays anchored and will be properly freed later. If submission fails, `usb_unanchor_urb` removes the anchor to avoid leaking the URB reference. This matches the pattern used in similar drivers (e.g., `gs_usb`) and correctly handles the memory leak.

